🧠 Test Idea: Process Creation and Timing Test
🎯 Goal

Understand:

How Linux creates a new process (fork() and exec()).

How long it takes to create and finish a process.

What happens when you create many processes quickly.

This test is simple conceptually but touches real Linux internals — process management, scheduling, and timing.

🧩 What You’ll Do

Create a child process using fork().

The child will run a simple command like ls or echo.

Measure how long it takes from before the fork to after the child finishes.

Repeat that process many times (e.g. 100 or 1000 loops).

Compute the average process creation + completion time.

📈 What You’re Measuring

The latency of process creation and termination on your system.

The consistency (or variability) — some forks may take longer if your system is busy.

This gives you insight into:

CPU scheduling.

Process table overhead.

The cost difference between processes and threads (later, you can compare).

🧾 Expected Output

You’ll print something like:

Test: Process creation time
Number of iterations: 1000
Average fork+exec+wait time: 1.35 milliseconds
Minimum: 1.12 ms
Maximum: 2.08 ms


